{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Protected reefs exhibit more even species‐abundance distributions than unprotected reefs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify and compare species‐abundance evenness among reefs under different protection statuses.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef‐year, aggregate abundance (or biomass) per species. 2. Compute Shannon diversity H' and species richness S, then Pielou’s evenness J = H'/ln(S). 3. Merge evenness values with protection_status for each reef‐year. 4. Fit a linear mixed‐effects model: evenness ~ protection_status + (1|id_reef). 5. Perform post‐hoc pairwise comparisons (Tukey) among protection categories. 6. Plot boxplots or violin plots of evenness by protection_status.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Mixed‐effects model summary of protection_status effect on evenness. 2. Pairwise comparison table with adjusted p‐values. 3. Boxplot/violin plot of evenness across protection categories."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Deep‐water strata support fish assemblages with lower species richness but higher evenness and biomass-per-species ratio compared to shallow strata.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate how depth strata influence richness, evenness and biomass-per-species at the reef scale.",  
      "steps":"1. Classify each transect by depth_stratum (e.g. shallow vs deep). 2. For each reef‐stratum‐year, calculate species richness, Pielou’s evenness, and biomass_per_species = total biomass / richness. 3. Fit three separate mixed‐effects models: richness ~ depth_stratum + (1|id_reef), evenness ~ depth_stratum + (1|id_reef), biomass_per_species ~ depth_stratum + (1|id_reef). 4. Extract model estimates and test depth_stratum effect. 5. Visualize means (±SE) of each response by depth_stratum.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Model summaries showing depth_stratum coefficients. 2. Estimated marginal means for shallow vs deep strata. 3. Multi‐panel plot of richness, evenness, and biomass_per_species by stratum."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Species evenness across all reefs has declined steadily from 2010 to 2020, indicating increasing dominance by a few species.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Detect temporal trends in community evenness over the survey period.",  
      "steps":"1. Compute Pielou’s evenness J for each reef‐year as in Experiment 1. 2. Aggregate one evenness value per reef‐year. 3. Fit a linear mixed‐effects model: evenness ~ year + (1|id_reef). 4. Check for autocorrelation and include AR(1) correlation structure if needed. 5. Test whether the slope for year is significantly negative. 6. Plot evenness vs year with regression line and confidence band.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Mixed‐effects model summary with year effect. 2. Diagnostic plots for model fit and residual autocorrelation. 3. Time‐series plot of evenness (2010–2020) with trend line."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs with higher species richness in 2010 experienced smaller proportional richness losses during the 2014–2016 warm anomaly than low‐richness reefs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether initial richness confers resilience to species loss during a climate disturbance.",  
      "steps":"1. Calculate species richness per reef in 2010 (R_2010). 2. Compute richness in the event period (2014–2016) for each reef (R_event) and proportional change = (R_event – R_2010)/R_2010. 3. Fit a linear regression: proportional_change ~ R_2010. 4. Assess whether richer reefs show less negative change (positive slope). 5. Plot proportional change vs R_2010 with regression line.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Regression model summary of proportional change on initial richness. 2. Scatterplot with best‐fit line and confidence interval. 3. Table of summary statistics for richness 2010 and proportional change."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Regional location explains more variation in reef‐scale community composition than protection status.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Partition variance in species composition attributable to region vs protection_status.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef‐year, build a species‐by‐reef presence‐absence or biomass matrix. 2. Compute Bray–Curtis dissimilarity among reef‐years. 3. Conduct PERMANOVA: dissimilarity ~ region + protection_status, with strata = id_reef to constrain permutations. 4. Evaluate R² and p‐values for region and protection_status. 5. Visualize the multivariate space with ordination (e.g. NMDS), coloring points by region and shaping by protection.",  
      "deliverables":"1. PERMANOVA table with R² for each factor. 2. NMDS ordination plot annotated by region and protection_status. 3. Interpretation of which factor is the stronger structuring force."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Temporal turnover (year‐to‐year community dissimilarity) is higher in unprotected reefs than in marine reserves.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare community stability across protection statuses using consecutive‐year dissimilarities.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef, for each consecutive year pair (e.g. 2010–2011, 2011–2012, …), compute Bray–Curtis dissimilarity of species abundances or biomass. 2. Create a table of turnover values with associated protection_status. 3. Fit a mixed‐effects model: turnover ~ protection_status + (1|id_reef). 4. Perform post‐hoc tests between statuses. 5. Plot turnover distributions by protection_status.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Mixed‐effects model summary testing protection_status effect. 2. Post‐hoc contrast table. 3. Boxplot or violin plot of year‐to‐year turnover by protection_status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The biomass contribution of the top five most abundant species per reef‐year has increased from 2010 to 2020, signaling growing dominance by a few species.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify changes in dominance by the top five species over time.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef‐year, rank species by biomass and sum biomass of the top five. 2. Compute dominance_ratio = (sum_biomass_top5) / (total_biomass). 3. Fit a mixed‐effects model: dominance_ratio ~ year + (1|id_reef). 4. Test whether the slope of year is significantly positive. 5. Plot dominance_ratio vs year with regression line.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Model summary showing temporal slope for dominance_ratio. 2. Time series plot of dominance_ratio (2010–2020). 3. Table of average dominance_ratio by decade (2010–2014 vs 2015–2020)."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The positive relationship between species richness and total biomass is stronger in protected reefs than in unprotected reefs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether protection status modifies the richness–biomass coupling.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef‐year, calculate species richness and total biomass. 2. Merge with protection_status. 3. Fit a mixed‐effects model: biomass ~ richness * protection_status + (1|id_reef). 4. Examine interaction term to assess whether slope of richness differs by status. 5. Plot biomass vs richness with separate regression lines for each protection_status.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Mixed‐effects model summary highlighting interaction coefficient. 2. ANCOVA table comparing slopes. 3. Scatterplot with fitted lines by protection_status."  
    }  
  }  
]}
